over the years i've always taken the  position that basically nothing need be  done  that  you know there is a uh  i'm not a i'm an agnostic religiously  which is say what can you show me  position  but i've always had the feeling that the  universe was put together in such a way  that it would work out all right  in the end  and have from my days  in the berkeley underground  i've  got a very healthy suspicion of leaders  and plans i mean i'm even more caustic  on the subject of political leaders than  i am on the subject of gurus  but nevertheless well this is a subject  which is very hot in our household which  is is the world getting better or is it  getting worse  and is there anything  that we personally  can do about it  and because it's always been my feeling  that it was all taken care of from  hyperspace  and that it would all make sense in the  end when the historical culmination  became visible to all the participants  i've taken the position that  the real political frontier is ourselves  reconstructing our attitudes uh molding  ourselves to be as caring loving and  conscious as we wish  that our leaders would be  nevertheless uh the culture crisis is  intensifying there's plenty of evidence  that things are  getting worse  in a hurry things like what i talked  about last night the burning of the  amazon the rise of the greenhouse effect  so forth and so on  so  it seems to me there are political  options there are  positions that need to be taken that are  not being taken  there are answers  even if  you are not a transcendentalist even if  you don't believe in the inevitable  working out of human history to the  satisfaction of everyone  and uh  when you begin to talk politics you  inevitably brush  the subject of responsibility  and permission  what kind of responsibilities  do we have to each other to the planet  to the future  what kind of permission  can we take from each other  from the planet  what is to be done and what can be done  and i wanted to  talk a little bit about this this  morning some ideas that we'll throw out  basically to get your feedback it's a  little different take  than  the psychedelic ranger sort of position  that you may be used to hearing  the psychedelic movement of the 1960s  uh  had no political  agenda really  i mean tim leary's agenda was turn on  tune in drop out  and the war  was a side issue where the new left made  common cause with the psychedelic people  and the war was eventually ended but the  psychedelic movement went nowhere except  underground and i think this was largely  because they disdain  to set  real-world political goals that  non-psychedelic people and let's face it  probably 95  at least if the human race is  non-psychedelic  non-psychedelic people  needed an agenda to relate to  and even in my own life i  have a kind of a tendency not to want to  dabble in politics not to want to  propose concrete solutions because they  are easily subverted easily attacked  they  create factionalism  which i think is a bad thing but  nevertheless i think there are  arenas and theaters of political action  that are not being articulated that we  could take more seriously and i want to  talk about a few of them fairly briefly  and then hopefully we can have a  dialogue about this  the first question being is it necessary  at all  to have a political agenda are we not  perhaps becoming frightened by the  intensifying of the historical crisis to  the point where we doubt our own  previous point of view  or  if you think it is necessary to have a  political agenda then what sort of  agenda should it be and i want to start  with the most immediate  and practical possibilities first and  then move towards some of the more  long-range  and globally  impactful possibilities  last night we talked about the  destruction of the rain forest in brazil  a very obvious political first step to  halting what is going on with the  destruction of nature in the third world  is  to get the international lending  community  to  exercise a degree of moral  responsibility  in other words it's a shocking notion to  suggest that the international monetary  fund  only support ecologically sound  projects  and yet it seems to me obvious that this  should be done  it isn't the brazilian government that  should be blamed for  the clearing of the amazon  or the land-hungry brazilian peasantry  it's the imf and the world bank because  they pay for it they make the  multi-billion dollar loans that make  this kind of thing possible  banks are amenable to political pressure  if their dirty laundry were shrillly  laundered in public  they would have a public relations  problem that would force them to rethink  what they are doing  there was an article in the new york  times about five days ago about how  world capitalism big capitalism is  finally actually alarmed by the  greenhouse effect because it's hitting  their investment  we live in the world but there are  agencies that think they own it  and uh when they see that their  investment is threatened and that's how  they analyze it these people don't love  wild animals and  climaxed rainforests they love money  and when they see their investment  threatened i would think they could be  pressured to  revise their loan policies  another step that it seems to me  lies directly ahead of us in the future  that i do not understand why it isn't  more discussed and can only conclude  somewhat light-heartedly because i don't  like being a paranoid that there must be  a conspiracy  against what i am about to propose a  conspiracy run by governments and that  is  the international boycott  is potentially a tremendous tool for  problem solving  but to date it has been so  gutted and watered down that it becomes  no more than a gesture the world today  is a global whole  it is very tightly  webbed together and spun into itself one  part into another  let's take the case of south africa  which i am hope with this crowd is a  fairly uncontroversial case  everyone is waving their arms saying  something must be done about south  africa it's a barbarian situation it's  intolerable  and the notion of a boycott as presently  practiced is that general motors will  take 18 months to cut its investment  there in half  and then this is hailed as a great step  forward  but in fact  the  uh the financial and political machinery  is in place to make life hell  for the government of south africa if  you are serious about changing the  policies of government you don't  threaten to withdraw your investment  over four years  you say  look  there will be no mail. there will be no  telephones there will be no  international banking transactions  you're through we're just pulling the  plug on this scene  and i guarantee you you would get action  the there is no reason why mail should  be delivered to that country there is no  reason why telephone service should be  maintained with these people and it is  inexcusable to loan them a single dime  obviously and you know choose  whatever problems scattered around the  world irk you particularly northern  ireland  israel  iran iraq war  uh  territorial encroachments going on in  africa if these people were just told no  money no phone no mail no trade nothing  it would uh and i believe that this  isn't done because every government  fears that if the machinery to apply  tools like this were in place it might  eventually be their turn  and no country wants to face that  because that's serious you can't go  forward they're just calling in the  chips when they do that so this is  something which rather than feeling  totally powerless we should all advocate  the tremendous strengthening of the  notion of international boycotts this is  very useful not a shot needs to be fired  and it goes without saying weaponry  should be withheld from these people  spare parts technology  engineers these south africans should  not be sending their people to our  engineering schools in fact they  shouldn't their passports shouldn't be  honored anywhere they should be invited  to go home and live in their wonderful  society if they think it's so great  and they can forget about phoning new  york or getting mail from new york and  paris but you're suggesting this uh  originally here as an ecological as a  technique for leaning on the situations  that are laying waste  i think it can be applied to like the  brazilian situation the south african  situation  wars wars cannot be fought without an  influx of capital materiel and  information  it's obviously something that people  would favor that governments would  oppose and so we've i've never heard any  politician of any stripe from gorbachev  to bush suggests that the strengthening  of the machinery of international  boycotts would be an obvious first step  toward putting this planet on a  reasonable course  [Music]  you
 

